  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 139 AM CDT Wed Oct 21 2026\n\nA relatively quiet weather pattern is expected across South Texas \nthrough much of the forecast period as mid-level ridging builds over \nthe region. Temperatures will remain near to slightly above seasonal \nnormals, with limited opportunities for precipitation. \n\nA weak disturbance will continue to support isolated to scattered \nshowers across portions of the Coastal Plains and adjacent waters \ntoday, with the greatest rain chances remaining offshore. As the \ndisturbance shifts away from the region and ridging becomes \nestablished, subsidence will favor predominantly dry conditions \nheading into the weekend.\n\nLittle change is anticipated through early next week as the ridge \nmaintains its influence across South Texas. Temperatures will \ncontinue to trend near to above normal, with generally light to \nmoderate winds. Overall, confidence remains fairly high in a \ncontinuation of the warm and mostly dry pattern through the middle \nof next week. Looking ahead, towards the end of the forecast, early \nmodel runs show another cold front sweeping across the region with \nanother potential cooldown and perhaps some showers, but more on \nthat later. \n\nGuidance continues to show elevated tidal levels and the potential \nfor minor coastal flooding continues through the week. This will be \naccompanied by a moderate risk of rip currents today mainly due to \nelevated swells around 8 seconds.",
